From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5sd <ps5sd@localhost>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] server: load another model without stopping the server

A model switch stopped sd-server and started it again with the new model.
For the time of the load (4 to 10 s on the console, a minute for some) nothing
answered on the port: the web UI lost the server, an event stream ended, and
a client could not wait for the switch on the connection that asked for it,
since that connection died with the server.

sd_server() now serves until sd_server_terminate(), with a model or without
one (--standby, which reads the model's options and does not use them), and
sd_server_load() loads another model into it while it serves, or none with
--standby. The model, its options and the generation defaults are a slot of
their own (ModelSlot) that the load replaces; the old model is freed before
the new one loads, as before, since both take from the console's one memory
pool. The routes that use the model hold a lock on it, shared, from the
match of the route to the end of the handler (install_model_gate); the job
worker holds it for each job; a load holds it alone. So a load waits for the
generation that runs, and a request that needs the model waits for the load:
a job queued meanwhile stays queued and runs on the new model. With no model
loaded those routes answer 503 standby, and a queued job fails with that code.
A cancel takes the context under a mutex of its own, so it never waits for a
load. The separate standby server (0056) is gone.

Measured with the library build and a driver on the PC: the port answered all
through a load; a load asked for while a job ran waited for it (9.8 s), the job
queued meanwhile ran on the new model; an event stream stayed open across two
loads; a load that failed left the server in standby, and the next one worked.

